# Build output
build/
*.o
*.a
*.exe
build.log
cmake.log
cmake_configure.log

# Vendored single-header deps (fetched at build time — see third_party/README.md)
third_party/httplib.h
third_party/nlohmann/

# Local config (copy from config/bootstrap.conf.example)
config/bootstrap.conf

# Compiled binaries from the hand-rolled crypto experiments under lib/.
# The sources are *.cpp/*.h; the extensionless build outputs must not be committed.
lib/sha1
lib/sha256
lib/test_ecc
lib/test_rsa

# Editor / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store

# demo target descriptor (holds provisioned creds), and the CMP client config downloaded
# beside it — provision-target.sh substitutes the live PBM secret into it, so it is a
# credential too, and it is regenerated on every provision.
demo/.target.env
demo/fastpki-cmp.cnf

# per-node / per-deployment compose overrides; template is .env.example
deploy/.env
deploy/k8s/env.local

# Key material and throwaway CA artifacts, ignored as a CLASS.
#
# This was two anchored lines — /ca.key and /ca.pem — naming the exact two files a test
# had leaked into the repo root at the time. Everything they did not name then accumulated
# unignored: seven private keys (four OCSP responder keys, two demo client keys and a
# certbot ACME account key), plus their certificates, CSRs, serial and extension files,
# sat in the work tree one `git add -A` away from being published. `git add -A` is already
# forbidden, but a rule somebody has to remember is not a control.
#
# Ignoring the class is deliberate and safe: a certificate authority's own repository has
# no business carrying a private key, and nothing tracked today does. The fuzz corpus is
# the one exception — tracked ASN.1 inputs, no key material — so it is negated below.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS THE PASSIVE HALF. An ignored secret is still a secret on the disk, and
# .gitignore only stops it being committed. tests/no_loose_secrets.sh is the half that
# FAILS when key material appears in the tree at all.
*.key
*.pem
*.crt
*.csr
*.srl
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
!fuzz/corpus/**

# Local Postgres-linked build tree (machine-local, like build/)
# Every side-by-side build tree, not a list of the ones that existed when this was
# written: the LDAP and Kerberos features are OFF in the default build/, so those
# variants are how their code gets compiled at all, and each new one was invisible to
# git status until somebody remembered to add a line here.
build-*/

# Our own 3-DC lab plumbing. Operational, not product: it hardcodes this lab's NetBird
# addresses, container names and repo path, and the topology conninfo carries the
# replication password. Kept locally (and Syncthing-versioned across both machines),
# never pushed. The mesh topology FORMAT is documented where a reader would look for
# it — the header of src/tools/mesh.cpp.
#
# The CONTENTS are ignored rather than the directory, so the one file that documents the
# format for an outside reader can be tracked. Ignoring `deploy/lab/` outright would make
# that impossible: git does not descend into an ignored directory, so a negation for a
# file inside one never takes effect.
deploy/lab/*
!deploy/lab/nodes.env.example

# The ACME bench cell places its webroot here when the temp dir is not shareable
# with docker (Docker Desktop). It is removed at the end of a run.
.bench-webroot/

# OpenTofu / Terraform working state for the cloud modules. The .tfvars a deployment
# actually uses is per-deployment (its region, its admin CIDRs, its AMI id) and the state
# describes live infrastructure — neither belongs to every clone. The committed
# *.tfvars.example is the template.
deploy/cloud/**/.terraform/
deploy/cloud/**/.terraform.lock.hcl
deploy/cloud/**/terraform.tfstate
deploy/cloud/**/terraform.tfstate.*
deploy/cloud/**/terraform.tfvars
deploy/cloud/**/tfplan
deploy/cloud/image/manifest.json
!deploy/cloud/**/terraform.tfvars.example
deploy/cloud/image/.fastpki-src.tar.gz

# Written at run time by demo/dns-auth.sh (the local dns-01 path mounts it into
# CoreDNS). Regenerated on every challenge; never edited by hand.
demo/coredns/
deploy/cloud/image/output-qemu*

# Generated by docs/publish.sh — the guides as pages and PDFs for the website.
docs/.publish/
docs/.out/
